1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,570
If AI can write code faster than you,

2
00:00:02,570 --> 00:00:05,350
build applications in minutes, and solve problems that

3
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:07,840
once took programmers days, then why would anyone

4
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:11,300
bother learning programming anymore? Um, it's a reasonable

5
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:13,500
question, and if you've spent any time on

6
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:16,530
the internet recently, you've probably heard the same

7
00:00:16,530 --> 00:00:19,550
prediction over and over again. Programming is dead,

8
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:22,600
AI will replace developers, there's no point learning

9
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:25,450
to code anymore. But I think we're looking

10
00:00:25,450 --> 00:00:27,850
at this completely backward, because the more powerful

11
00:00:27,850 --> 00:00:30,560
AI becomes, the more valuable a particular kind

12
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:34,000
of programming knowledge might become. And strangely enough,

13
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,060
it has very little to do with writing

14
00:00:36,060 --> 00:00:39,060
code. To understand why, we need to rethink

15
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:42,100
what programming actually is. For decades, we've treated

16
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:45,200
programming primarily as a technical skill. You learn

17
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:48,560
a language, you memorize syntax, you understand loops,

18
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:51,850
functions, classes, and data structures, and eventually, you

19
00:00:51,850 --> 00:00:54,720
learn how to turn instructions into working software.

20
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:56,880
The better you become at writing those instructions,

21
00:00:56,880 --> 00:00:59,560
the better programming you're supposed to be. But

22
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:02,470
something interesting is happening. AI is making the

23
00:01:02,470 --> 00:01:05,620
act of writing code dramatically easier. Tasks that

24
00:01:05,620 --> 00:01:09,440
previously required hours of searching documentation, debugging, and

25
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:12,400
writing boilerplate can sometimes be completed in a

26
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:15,040
few prompts, and this changes the economics of

27
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:17,780
programming. When the cost of producing code goes

28
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:20,110
down, knowing what code should do becomes more

29
00:01:20,110 --> 00:01:22,960
important. Think about that for a moment. If

30
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:26,090
almost anyone can generate an application, what separates

31
00:01:26,090 --> 00:01:28,900
a useful application from a useless one? It's

32
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:30,810
no longer just the ability to write the

33
00:01:30,810 --> 00:01:33,000
code, it's the ability to recognize the right

34
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,420
problem, to understand how different pieces connect, to

35
00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:39,050
anticipate what could go wrong, and to design

36
00:01:39,050 --> 00:01:41,770
a solution that actually makes sense. This is

37
00:01:41,770 --> 00:01:44,670
where programming starts becoming something much bigger than

38
00:01:44,670 --> 00:01:46,740
a technical skill. It becomes a way of

39
00:01:46,740 --> 00:01:50,800
thinking. Consider systems thinking. When you learn programming,

40
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:52,470
you gradually stop seeing the world as a

41
00:01:52,470 --> 00:01:56,330
collection of isolated tasks. Instead, you begin seeing

42
00:01:56,330 --> 00:02:01,360
systems, inputs, outputs, dependencies, feedback loops, constraints, and

43
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:04,920
hidden relationships. Imagine you're running a small business,

44
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:09,520
you receive customer requests, organize information, update spreadsheets,

45
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:12,880
generate invoices, and send follow-up emails. To most

46
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:15,350
people, these might appear to be five different

47
00:02:15,350 --> 00:02:17,690
jobs that someone with a programming mindset might

48
00:02:17,690 --> 00:02:22,250
see something else. One interconnected system. Information enters

49
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:24,290
at one point, moves through a series of

50
00:02:24,290 --> 00:02:27,320
transformations and produces an outcome, and once you

51
00:02:27,320 --> 00:02:30,990
recognize that structure, an entirely different question becomes

52
00:02:30,990 --> 00:02:33,760
possible. Why am I repeating these steps manually?

53
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:36,790
Why isn't this automated? Why does this information

54
00:02:36,790 --> 00:02:38,920
need to be entered twice? And what would

55
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:41,780
happen if I redesigned the entire workflow? That's

56
00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:45,480
the unreasonable effectiveness of programming. Not because programming

57
00:02:45,480 --> 00:02:47,970
magically makes you smarter, but because it teaches

58
00:02:47,970 --> 00:02:50,080
you to recognize structures that are easy to

59
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:52,860
overlook, and once you recognize a structure, you

60
00:02:52,860 --> 00:02:55,650
can often improve it. This leads to another

61
00:02:55,650 --> 00:02:59,520
important idea, optimization. A programmer eventually learns that

62
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:02,200
making something work is only the beginning. You

63
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:04,570
can write a program that produces the correct

64
00:03:04,570 --> 00:03:07,000
answer. But what happens when the amount of

65
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,940
data increases a thousand times? What happens when

66
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:12,460
ten users become ten million? What happens when

67
00:03:12,460 --> 00:03:15,230
a process that costs one dollar suddenly costs

68
00:03:15,230 --> 00:03:17,820
a thousand? These questions force you to think

69
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:23,440
about efficiency, time complexity, memory consumption, resources, trade-offs,

70
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:27,080
and perhaps most importantly, scalability. But this kind

71
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:29,050
of thinking doesn't have to stay inside a

72
00:03:29,050 --> 00:03:31,370
computer. You start applying it to your daily

73
00:03:31,370 --> 00:03:34,480
life. You notice unnecessary steps in your workflow.

74
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:37,760
You recognize patterns in repetitive decisions. You begin

75
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:41,210
asking whether an activity could be simplified, delegated,

76
00:03:41,210 --> 00:03:45,440
automated, or eliminated entirely. And suddenly, programming isn't

77
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:48,330
just helping you build software. It's changing how

78
00:03:48,330 --> 00:03:51,360
you approach problems. Now, here's where AI makes

79
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:54,780
this even more interesting. Imagine two people using

80
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:57,440
exactly the same AI model. The first person

81
00:03:57,440 --> 00:04:00,260
asks it to build a productivity application. The

82
00:04:00,260 --> 00:04:03,330
AI generates some code, creates an interface, and

83
00:04:03,330 --> 00:04:06,790
produces something that works. That's already impressive. But

84
00:04:06,790 --> 00:04:09,950
the second person approaches the problem differently. They

85
00:04:09,950 --> 00:04:12,900
ask. Who is this application actually for? What

86
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:15,610
problem does it solve? What information needs to

87
00:04:15,610 --> 00:04:17,820
be stored? What happens when the user makes

88
00:04:17,820 --> 00:04:21,090
a mistake? Which operations are expensive? What happens

89
00:04:21,090 --> 00:04:23,600
when the system grows? And which features shouldn't

90
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:25,720
exist in the first place? Both people have

91
00:04:25,720 --> 00:04:28,100
access to the same intelligence. But they're using

92
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:30,760
it with different levels of structural understanding. The

93
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:33,600
difference isn't necessarily who can write better code.

94
00:04:33,680 --> 00:04:36,560
It's who can ask better questions, recognize better

95
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:39,680
trade-offs, and evaluate the answers. And programming is

96
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:41,580
one of the disciplines that can help develop

97
00:04:41,580 --> 00:04:43,850
those abilities. This is why I think we're

98
00:04:43,850 --> 00:04:46,850
witnessing a transition. Programming is moving from being

99
00:04:46,850 --> 00:04:49,990
primarily a specialized production skill toward becoming a

100
00:04:49,990 --> 00:04:52,970
broader thinking skill. Not that the technical knowledge

101
00:04:52,970 --> 00:04:56,800
disappears, it still matters enormously, especially when building

102
00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:59,630
reliable, secure, or complex systems. But the value

103
00:04:59,630 --> 00:05:01,900
of programming is expanding beyond the people who

104
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:05,680
write software professionally. A researcher who understands programming

105
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:09,120
can automate experiments. A designer can prototype ideas

106
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:12,020
without waiting for an engineering team. A creator

107
00:05:12,020 --> 00:05:15,360
can build tools that eliminate repetitive production work.

108
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:17,930
An entrepreneur can test an idea before investing

109
00:05:17,930 --> 00:05:20,790
thousands of dollars into development. The value isn't

110
00:05:20,790 --> 00:05:23,350
simply in creating more software, it's in having

111
00:05:23,350 --> 00:05:25,130
the ability to turn an idea into a

112
00:05:25,130 --> 00:05:28,190
working system. And now, with AI, that ability

113
00:05:28,190 --> 00:05:31,380
becomes accessible to many more people. But there's

114
00:05:31,380 --> 00:05:33,410
one more part of this conversation that I

115
00:05:33,410 --> 00:05:36,760
think we're missing. Human understanding. AI can already

116
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:40,310
do extraordinary things. It can generate code, analyze

117
00:05:40,310 --> 00:05:45,290
complicated systems, propose architectures, discover patterns, and sometimes

118
00:05:45,290 --> 00:05:47,920
produce ideas that are more creative or effective

119
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:50,570
than anything we would have considered ourselves. I

120
00:05:50,570 --> 00:05:53,360
don't think we should underestimate that. In fact,

121
00:05:53,360 --> 00:05:56,560
I think these capabilities will continue to improve,

122
00:05:56,560 --> 00:05:59,130
but there's an important distinction between solving a

123
00:05:59,130 --> 00:06:01,850
specified problem and deciding which problem deserves to

124
00:06:01,850 --> 00:06:04,670
be solved. AI can reason about human needs

125
00:06:04,670 --> 00:06:07,310
when we provide context, but it doesn't live

126
00:06:07,310 --> 00:06:10,570
your life. It doesn't experience your daily frustrations

127
00:06:10,570 --> 00:06:14,080
firsthand. It doesn't have your relationships, your responsibilities,

128
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:16,650
where your personal reasons for caring about a

129
00:06:16,650 --> 00:06:19,000
particular outcome. And it doesn't bear the consequences

130
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:20,370
of a decision in the same way you

131
00:06:20,370 --> 00:06:22,470
do. You are the one who has to

132
00:06:22,470 --> 00:06:25,620
decide what matters, what is worth building, which

133
00:06:25,620 --> 00:06:28,760
compromises are acceptable. And when a technically impressive

134
00:06:28,760 --> 00:06:31,730
solution is actually the wrong solution, that's where

135
00:06:31,730 --> 00:06:35,060
human judgment becomes essential. And that's why understanding

136
00:06:35,060 --> 00:06:37,210
programming is so useful, because it gives you

137
00:06:37,210 --> 00:06:40,160
a language for translating human intentions into systems

138
00:06:40,160 --> 00:06:42,040
that can act on them. Now, I don't

139
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:45,140
want to pretend that nothing is changing. Some

140
00:06:45,140 --> 00:06:48,640
programming tasks are absolutely becoming cheaper, some jobs

141
00:06:48,640 --> 00:06:51,760
will change significantly, and simply knowing the syntax

142
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:54,090
of a programming language may no longer provide

143
00:06:54,090 --> 00:06:56,200
the advantage it once did. But that doesn't

144
00:06:56,200 --> 00:06:59,350
mean learning programming has become pointless. It means

145
00:06:59,350 --> 00:07:01,690
the reason to learn it is changing. Maybe

146
00:07:01,690 --> 00:07:04,160
the future isn't about becoming the person who

147
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:06,090
can write the most code. Maybe it's about

148
00:07:06,090 --> 00:07:08,490
becoming the person who understands what needs to

149
00:07:08,490 --> 00:07:10,670
be built, why it matters, and how all

150
00:07:10,670 --> 00:07:13,130
the pieces should work together. Someone who can

151
00:07:13,130 --> 00:07:15,050
look at a complicated problem and break it

152
00:07:15,050 --> 00:07:18,640
into smaller, understandable parts. Someone who can recognize

153
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:22,000
inefficiency, someone who understands systems well enough to

154
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,660
guide increasingly powerful AI tools, and someone who

155
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:27,130
can tell the difference between software that merely

156
00:07:27,130 --> 00:07:29,841
works and software that's actually useful. So if

157
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:32,410
you're wondering whether programming is still worth learning

158
00:07:32,410 --> 00:07:34,670
in the age of AI, I would argue

159
00:07:34,670 --> 00:07:37,250
that it is. But don't learn programming just

160
00:07:37,250 --> 00:07:40,240
to memorize syntax. Learn it to understand systems,

161
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:42,800
learn it to recognize patterns, learn it to

162
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:46,360
design, question, and optimize. Learn it because even

163
00:07:46,360 --> 00:07:48,880
if AI eventually writes nearly all the code,

164
00:07:48,970 --> 00:07:51,450
someone still needs to understand what we're trying

165
00:07:51,450 --> 00:07:53,570
to accomplish. And the better you understand how

166
00:07:53,570 --> 00:07:55,920
things work, the more possibilities you can imagine.

167
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:59,130
Programming isn't just about telling computers what to

168
00:07:59,130 --> 00:08:01,460
do, it's about learning how to turn complexity

169
00:08:01,460 --> 00:08:04,640
into something understandable, and ideas into something real.

170
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:07,920
Maybe that's why, in a world where writing

171
00:08:07,920 --> 00:08:10,180
code is becoming easier than ever, the ability

172
00:08:10,180 --> 00:08:12,120
to think like a programmer could be worth

173
00:08:12,120 --> 00:08:12,880
more than ever.

